<Hook>
  You hold As Ad. Your opponent holds Ks Kh. The board is 2c 3d 7h 9s, and only
  the river remains. Aces currently lead, but which river cards can change the
  winner? First compare the hands correctly; then the simulation can count the
  relevant river cards.
</Hook>

<Idea>
### Compare five cards, in order

The hand category comes first. Every flush beats every straight, even if the straight has higher individual ranks. Within a category, compare the ranks that define the category. If those tie, compare the leftover ranks, called **kickers**, from highest to lowest. Stop at the first difference. Never use suit as a tiebreaker.

For one pair, compare the pair's rank, then the three kickers. For two pair, compare the higher pair, then the lower pair, then the kicker. For three of a kind, compare the triple and then the two kickers. A full house compares the triple before the pair; four of a kind compares the four matching cards before its kicker. A straight compares its top rank; the wheel A-2-3-4-5 has five at the top. Flushes and high-card hands compare their five ranks in descending order.

<RankingTable />

A royal flush is the ace-high straight flush, rather than an extra category above straight flush. The categories above rank five-card hands. Chapter 6 will count how often they occur; their ordering does not change when more cards are available.

### The board can play

Suppose the board itself is As Ks Qs Js Ts. Its royal flush is the best five for every remaining player. The hole cards cannot improve it. The pot is split among the tied winners. Your extra unused card is not a sixth-card kicker.

Similarly, a private pair can stop appearing in your best five when the board supplies stronger pairs. Always rebuild the best five from the available cards, rather than treating your original hand's name as permanent.

### The button is a position

With three or more players, the small blind sits immediately left of the button and the big blind is next. Preflop action starts left of the big blind. After the flop, action starts with the first active player left of the button. The button therefore acts late after the flop, seeing other actions before deciding.

With two players, the button posts the small blind and acts first preflop. The big blind acts first after the flop, so the button acts last then. Remember the two-player exception instead of applying a large-table seating picture mechanically.

### Count the river in our spot

Remove the four known hole cards and the four board cards. Each remaining card can be the river. In this particular spot, either remaining king makes the opponent three kings; every other river leaves the aces ahead. No river ties. The notebook counts winning river cards over all available river cards, and the simulation checks that count. This is a win probability for the stated, fully known matchup, not an estimate of an unseen opponent's range.

### A repeatable way to compare hands

When a showdown looks confusing, ignore the betting story for a moment. Select one player's best five cards and name their category. Then do the same for the other player. If the categories match, compare the defining ranks before comparing kickers. Stop as soon as one rank differs.

This order matters. A large unused hole card cannot rescue a weaker pair, and a pair's kicker cannot outrank an opponent's higher pair. You can practice by explaining which five cards the game selected and why the remaining cards were left out. Only after the comparison is clear should you ask how often that result could have happened.

<Shortcut />
</Idea>

